{"id":"5a6c62d4-e270-4cc7-83cc-9a2e7b2956de","kind":"note","ref":{"span":"32ca3768ae5b00fa","key":"bablr1","file":"packages/editor/extensions/capabilities/decisions.ts","baseline":{"blob":"478da735df8167b89f854947686934df6c0454ea","start":0,"end":75},"shape":{"type":"ImportDeclaration","atoms":["import","type","{","ObservedExtension","}","from","\"","@brianjenkins94/editor-api/runtime","\""]},"context":{"after":"3366a4fec56a7145"}},"payload":{"text":"How a capability call is decided, said on the call: the policy's answer for it and the rule that gives it (or that nothing\ndoes), how loud that should be, and the lightbulb's ways of changing it. There's no list of calls or rules to go and look\nat, since nobody does (the user, 2026-11-06). The whole list, for when it's wanted, is the policy file itself, with each\nrule's sentence above it.\n\nA call is decided as a run decides it (`effectiveDisposition`, the first rule that matches, my rules ahead of the shared\ncontract), so what's said here is what a run does: `review` is a stop.","wrote":["Brian Jenkins"]},"author":"local","createdAt":"2026-11-22T17:23:27.667Z","updatedAt":"2026-11-22T17:23:27.667Z"}
